# Cooked artifacts (mesh/texture cooks, SPIR-V shader cache) — machine-local, content-addressed
Cooked/

# ...with two deliberate exceptions, both of which are SOURCE that merely lives under a cooked path
# because that is the only directory the preloader scans for its kind.
#
# THE FIRST. A texture only becomes referenceable by a .demat once a .tex exists under Cooked/Textures/
# carrying its handle — and NOTHING IN THE EDITOR COOKS LOOSE TEXTURES. The startup cook walks
# Resources/Assets/Meshes/ and Resources/Assets/Collections/ only (EditorLayer's two ImportAllFromDirectory
# stages), so a .tex under Cooked/Textures/ exists for an image beside a mesh, or for one an artist dragged
# into the Content Browser by hand, and for nothing else. T_Checker.png is neither: it sits in
# Resources/Assets/Textures/ and is named by M_CheckerFloor.demat, which eleven shipped scenes use. On a
# fresh clone without this one file the checkerboard floor draws untextured and no cook ever repairs it.
# So it is committed because it is UNREPRODUCIBLE HERE, not because it is source.
#
# THE REASON THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO GIVE — "the drag-drop importer mints that handle from the texture's
# ABSOLUTE path", so a shipped .tex would need a frozen handle — HAS NOT BEEN TRUE SINCE FromCookedPath
# became project-relative. The handle is AssetHandle::FromCookedPath of the SOURCE image's place inside
# the project, identical on every machine, and TextureImporter re-derives and re-stamps it on every cook
# (it does NOT "preserve the handle of an existing .tex" either — that branch was deleted for freezing
# random-era ids). T_Checker.tex's stored handle equals its own derivation today, and TextureAsset::Load
# now says so loudly when a .tex's does not.
#
# WHICH IS WHY THE OTHER .tex FILES STAY IGNORED. They are pure cooks: every field is a function of the
# source image, the cook is deterministic and cheap, and committing one would put a second copy of a
# derived number in the repository — the exact thing that broke here. Four .tex files written before the
# derivation changed carried stale handles, and the two .demat naming them pointed at nothing on any
# machine that cooked from scratch. Desert/Tests/Engine/AssetReferenceCensus pins every material's
# references against the derivation instead, which needs no cooked file at all and so holds in CI.
#
# Re-included one directory level at a time, and the intermediate `/*` lines are load-bearing: git will
# not look inside a directory it has already excluded, so `!.../T_Checker.tex` alone matches nothing.
# Each level is re-ignored immediately after being opened, which is what keeps the other ~660 cooked
# files (shader cache, pipeline cache, mesh cooks) ignored.
!Editor/Cooked/
Editor/Cooked/*
!Editor/Cooked/Textures/
Editor/Cooked/Textures/*
!Editor/Cooked/Textures/T_Checker.tex

# THE SECOND: the skinned probe. Until 2026-09-04 this repository contained NOT ONE skinned mesh in any
# scene, which is why the skinned rendering path accumulated three independent defects that nothing could
# see — no cloud shadow, no cascade shadows and no ambient, and finally a mesh that could not be loaded
# from a scene at all. Each was found only when somebody hand-baked a rig, verified against it, and threw
# it away again. This pair is that rig, kept: 5 KB total, a one-bone rest pose and a 100 cm box weighted
# entirely to it, hand-authored rather than produced by any importer — re-running the cook would not
# recreate it, because no source .fbx exists for it. Editor/Resources/Assets/Scenes/MESH_SkinnedProbe.desce
# places it beside a static box of the same material, and Desert/Tests/Engine/SkinnedMeshDependency pins
# both derived numbers (the rig signature and the mesh's path-derived handle) so a rename fails loudly.
# THE THIRD: the STATIC probe, and it is here for the same reason one merge later. Until 2026-09-08 no
# scene in this repository named a `.stmesh` at all — the one that did (M10_MeshSlot) pointed at a 40 MB
# import nobody else has — so the cooked STATIC mesh path was as unobservable as the skinned one had been,
# and it was silently broken: MeshService::Register built the GPU mesh BEFORE the file was parsed, cached a
# mesh with zero submeshes under the handle, and every draw from then on issued nothing. 7 KB, two boxes of
# deliberately different shape (a 100 cm cube and a 100x200x100 pillar) so a frame that loses ONE submesh
# is visibly wrong rather than merely dimmer. Hand-authored, like the skinned pair: no source .fbx exists,
# so re-running the cook would not recreate it. Desert/Tests/Engine/StaticMeshCooked parses this exact file
# and pins what it carries; M10_MeshSlot.desce places it.
# THE FOURTH: the animation clips, and the same sentence again one kind later. Until 2026-09-09 this
# repository contained NOT ONE `.anim` file, so the clip half of the animation system — the scan, the
# library, the rig match, the Animator, the skinning matrices — had never once been exercised end to end
# from disk by anything a person could run. That is what let the library go UNFILLED in the packaged game
# for as long as it did: every character T-posed and no frame in the tree could show it, because no frame
# in the tree had a clip to play. Three files, 12 KB, hand-authored like the rigs above and not
# reproducible by any importer: SkinProbe_Hover translates the one bone 200 cm up and back, SkinProbe_Tilt
# turns it once about Z (a different KIND of motion, so a frame proves which clip played rather than only
# that something did), and Foreign_Hips is the NEGATIVE CONTROL — a different rig signature animating a
# bone name SkinProbe does not have, so a library that offers it to this rig is wrong in a way a passing
# frame would otherwise hide. Editor/Resources/Assets/Scenes/ANIM_ClipProbe.desce plays the first two;
# Desert/Tests/Engine/AnimationClipCorpus parses all three and pins what they carry.
# THE FIFTH: the TWO-BONE WITNESS, and it exists because of what the four above CANNOT see. Every rig in
# this repository until now was ONE bone with an IDENTITY OffsetMatrix and no parent chain, so a whole class
# of animation defect — anything that confuses a bone's own space with skinning space — produced EXACTLY the
# same frame as the correct code and was therefore unobservable here by any means. One such defect is live in
# `dev` as this lands: Animator::CalculateBlendedPose decomposes `global * OffsetMatrix` and slerps the
# rotation of THAT PRODUCT, i.e. of a quantity with the inverse bind pose baked into it. Measured over the
# two corpora: on the one-bone probe the wrong blend and the right one differ by 0.000 cm at every key pair
# and every alpha; on this rig they differ by up to 254.96 cm. TwoBoneProbe is two bones, "Base" (root) and
# "Arm" (its child), both with a non-identity OffsetMatrix and a non-identity parent transform; its mesh is
# two boxes, one per bone, so the chain is visible rather than inferred. Neither bone is named "Root" or
# "Hips" on purpose — ClipSkeletonMatch falls back to bone-name overlap, so either name would hand this rig
# the one-bone corpus or the Foreign_Hips negative control. Hand-authored like everything above, and
# Desert/Tests/Engine/TwoBoneWitness rebuilds the whole construction in C++ and compares it to these bytes.
# Editor/Resources/Assets/Scenes/ANIM_TwoBoneWitness.desce places it, blind control beside witness.
# THE SEVENTH: a PAIR of clips that differ in ONE field. A6Curve_Linear and A6Curve_Cubic carry the same
# three position keys on IKProbe's chain root — up to a peak and back — and differ only in each key's
# `Shape.Interp`. That is what makes the frame a statement about interpolation rather than about anything
# else: the two scenes that play them differ in one string, the keys are identical, and the pose at a frame
# sitting exactly ON a key must therefore be pixel-identical while a frame BETWEEN keys must not. The middle
# key is a peak on purpose — the auto pass makes an extremum flat, which is where a cubic and a straight
# line disagree most and where a curve editor's worst bug (sailing past the highest key) would show.
# Hand-authored; no importer produces them. Desert/Tests/Engine/KeyInterpolationWitness rebuilds both from
# their construction and checks the pair differs in exactly that one field.

# THE SIXTH: the IK PROBE, and it exists because the fifth one cannot carry the chain. A two-bone IK chain
# is THREE joints — the end bone, its parent and its grandparent — so TwoBoneProbe's two bones give ONE
# segment, which is a look-at and not IK. IKProbe is four bones: IK_Shoulder (root) -> IK_Elbow -> IK_Hand is
# the 80 cm / 60 cm chain, and IK_Post is a SECOND ROOT standing exactly at the goal position, never animated
# and never touched by the solver — the negative control inside the same picture, so "the bones the solver
# does not name did not move" is a rectangle of a frame rather than an argument. Every bind transform carries
# a rotation as well as a translation, for the reason the fifth entry gives. The names avoid Root/Hips/Base/
# Arm for the reason the fifth entry gives too. IKProbe_Swing swings the shoulder +-35 degrees about Z and the
# elbow +-20 about X over 2 s at 1 tick/s, so under `--play` frame 90 is t = 1.5 s by construction; with IK on,
# the hand stays on the post while the animation tries to swing it away, which is the whole demonstration.
# Hand-authored, no importer produces these bytes, and Desert/Tests/Engine/IKProbeRig rebuilds the entire
# construction in C++ and compares it to them. Editor/Resources/Assets/Scenes/ANIM_IKWitness.desce places five
# copies: no IK, alpha 1, alpha 0.5, alpha 0, and a second no-IK copy that measures whether two identical
# entities at different world positions render identically at all.
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/
Editor/Cooked/Meshes/*
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/SkinProbe.skeleton
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/SkinProbe.skmesh
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/StaticProbe.stmesh
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/SkinProbe_Hover.anim
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/SkinProbe_Tilt.anim
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/Foreign_Hips.anim
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/TwoBoneProbe.skeleton
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/TwoBoneProbe.skmesh
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/TwoBoneProbe_Wave.anim
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/TwoBoneProbe_Twist.anim
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/IKProbe.skeleton
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/IKProbe.skmesh
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/IKProbe_Swing.anim
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/A6Curve_Linear.anim
!Editor/Cooked/Meshes/A6Curve_Cubic.anim

